<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akaba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1716</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Houessou Akaba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akaba was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> from about 1685, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a>. He extended the kingdom's territory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau through wars with its neighbours. He died around 1716, and after a period in which, according to some traditions, his twin sister Hangbe ruled briefly, the throne passed to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">Agaja</a>, who carried Dahomey to the coast.</p></div>
